## Foundation in `src/app/index.css`

Don't duplicate; reuse:

- `.skip-to-content` — visually-hidden anchor revealed on focus (placed first in the DOM by `StreamOnlyWindow`).
- `.sr-only` — visually-hidden text for screen-reader-only labels.
- `:focus-visible { outline: 4px auto -webkit-focus-ring-color; outline-offset: 2px; }` — keyboard-only focus ring.
- `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` — global animation/transition shutoff, **with an `essential-motion` opt-out** (see pitfall §1 below).
- `.essential-motion` — opt-out class for indicators that MUST keep animating (loading spinners, recording pulses) even when the user prefers reduced motion. Apply to the animated element directly.

## Pitfalls (lessons from real bugs)

These are real regressions we hit while making the project WCAG-compliant. They share a theme: **WCAG fixes interact with the existing styling stack (Bootstrap reboot, browser UA, Chromium quirks) in non-obvious ways**. Always re-test affected UI after applying any of these patterns.

### 1. Reduced-motion blanket rules will freeze your loading spinners

The canonical recipe — `*, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: 0.001ms !important; ... }` — kills *every* animation, including indeterminate progress indicators. WCAG 2.3.3 only requires reducing **non-essential** motion. Loading spinners are essential per WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages): without motion the user can't tell the app isn't frozen.

Pattern:
- Keep the global kill switch in `index.css`.
- Add an `.essential-motion` exemption in the same `@media` block that re-asserts sane explicit values (`animation-duration: 1s !important; animation-iteration-count: infinite !important;`). **Do NOT use `revert`** — it reverts to the user-agent default (`0s` / `1`), which kills the animation just as effectively as the kill switch.
- Apply `className="essential-motion"` to the spinning element. Also add `role="progressbar"` + `aria-label` while you're there.
- Reference: `LoadingOverlay.tsx`, `SceneLoadingOverlay.tsx`.

### 2. Expanding a touch target makes the existing focus halo suddenly visible

The 44×44 touch-target trick (`border: 18px solid transparent` + negative margin + `background-clip: padding-box`) doesn't add a focus halo — it just **enlarges the element**, so the focus ring that was *always* being drawn (Bootstrap reboot's `:focus` `box-shadow`, the browser's `-webkit-focus-ring-color` UA ring) now paints around a 45×45 area instead of the original tiny 9×9, and the previously-invisible halo becomes obvious.

When using the transparent-border touch-target trick on `<button>`, also do all of:

```css
.your-dot {
    -webkit-appearance: none;
    appearance: none;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    touch-action: manipulation;
    background-clip: padding-box !important;   /* don't let any state reset this */
    background-image: none !important;
    border-color: transparent !important;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
    user-select: none;
}

.your-dot:hover,
.your-dot:focus,
.your-dot:active {
    outline: none !important;
    box-shadow: none !important;
    background-image: none !important;
    border-color: transparent !important;
    /* IMPORTANT: don't re-set background-color here. Re-asserting the same
       value retriggers any transition and can flash a paint on Chromium. */
}

.your-dot:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid <token> !important;
    outline-offset: 2px;
    box-shadow: none !important;
}

/* Touch-primary devices: hide the keyboard ring because some Chromium
   builds incorrectly match :focus-visible on tap, leaving a stuck halo
   on the just-tapped element. The state colour change is the indicator. */
@media (hover: none) and (pointer: coarse) {
    .your-dot:focus-visible {
        outline: none !important;
    }
}
```

And in the TSX, suppress focus-on-pointer with:

```tsx
<button
    onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()}   // mousedown is the spec-defined focus-killer
    onClick={...}
/>
```

`mousedown` (not `pointerdown`) is the only spec-defined way to skip focus on click. Touch synthesises `mousedown`, so this covers tap and double-tap. Keyboard activation goes through `keydown`, so Enter/Space + Arrow-key navigation still focus the element correctly.

Reference: `OnboardingOverlay.tsx` + `OnboardingOverlay.css` `.onboarding-dot`.

### 3. Bootstrap reboot leaks through

The project imports `bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css` globally. Bootstrap reboot adds `:focus` `box-shadow` and other styling to bare `<button>` and form elements. When killing focus visuals, you need `outline: none !important; box-shadow: none !important;` to defeat both the UA default ring and Bootstrap's reboot — author-level styles without `!important` lose to Bootstrap's specificity in some cases.

### 4. Re-asserting the same `background-color` on `:hover` can flash

If your element has `transition: background-color 0.25s ease` and you write a `:hover` rule that sets the same colour the element already has, Chromium can still trigger a transient repaint that — combined with `background-clip` mid-paint — looks like a halo flicker. Don't write hover rules just to "be defensive" if they don't change anything. Only restate properties on `:hover/:focus/:active` if you actually need to override an inherited value.

### 5. CU keyboard vs dev stream focus

**CU production** uses point-and-click / touch — **not** WASD (`controlMode === 'wasd'` is dev-only, `DevViewLayer` / Navigation submenu). When auditing CU Tab order, ignore WASD.

- `#remote-video` stays **`tabIndex={-1}`** (`AppStreamCore`) so the stream canvas is not a tab stop in CU.
- Dev WASD may set `tabIndex={0}` and refocus the video in `StreamOnlyWindow` only when `controlMode === 'wasd'`; refocus must **not** run when Tab moves to another control (`relatedTarget` must be empty).
- When a **foreground bottom sheet** stacks over an still-visible modal (Find my seat over Search), mark the background dialog **`inert`** and disable its focus trap — see `useSeatSheetForeground`.

## Localization & TTS cross-references

- **Localization**: `localization.mdc` — i18next setup, EN/SV/FR/ES, AI API en/sv constraint, `aiLanguageFor()` for outgoing AI requests.
- **Text-to-Speech**: `text-to-speech.mdc` — voice language vs text language, `AvatarVoiceProfile`, per-avatar voice data in `interactions.json`.

## Workflow when uncertain

1. Pick the closest existing pattern from the table above and read it before coding.
2. If the design needs something not in the table, decide: is there a WCAG-compliant way to express the intent? If yes, do that.
3. If no — **stop and tell the user**: "This design proposes X, which conflicts with WCAG criterion Y because Z. Options are A, B, C. Should I check with design before implementing?"
4. Don't ship a workaround that silently fails WCAG just to match a mockup.

